1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,707
For three and a half minutes, a perfectly working airliner fell out of the night sky toward the Atlantic Ocean.

2
00:00:06,707 --> 00:00:09,256
Its engines ran flawlessly all the way down.

3
00:00:09,256 --> 00:00:13,081
And the two pilots at the controls never understood they were falling.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,763
228 people took off from Rio de Janeiro, bound for Paris.

5
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:24,571
Somewhere over the middle of the ocean, beyond all radar, their aircraft simply vanished.

6
00:00:25,699 --> 00:00:37,350
For almost two years, the only witnesses were 24 automated maintenance messages, sent by the dying aircraft itself, to a computer in Paris that nobody was watching at 2 in the morning.

7
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,939
The wreck lay deeper than the Titanic.

8
00:00:40,939 --> 00:00:47,910
It took four search expeditions and robots from the team that found the Titanic to bring the black boxes back.

9
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:50,699
What was on them stunned the aviation world.

10
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:54,535
I don't have control of the airplane any more now.

11
00:00:54,535 --> 00:00:56,967
I don't have control of the airplane at all.

12
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,772
But he did have control.

13
00:00:59,772 --> 00:01:04,455
The airplane did everything he asked of it, all the way to the water.

14
00:01:04,455 --> 00:01:08,469
That is what makes this the most haunting crash in modern aviation.

15
00:01:08,469 --> 00:01:10,141
This is the full story.

16
00:01:11,266 --> 00:01:16,099
Titanic of the skies. The fall of Air France 447.

17
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:20,080
Chapter 1. The last flight out of Rio.

18
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:26,159
May 31st, 2009. Rio de Janeiro, Galeão airport.

19
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:32,979
Air France flight 447 boards for the overnight run to Paris.

20
00:01:32,979 --> 00:01:39,798
216 passengers from 32 countries. Honeymooners. Doctors. Three children under 12.

21
00:01:40,933 --> 00:01:43,026
The aircraft is this one.

22
00:01:43,026 --> 00:01:50,144
F-GZCP, an Airbus A330, four years old, one of the most advanced and safest airliners ever built.

23
00:01:50,144 --> 00:01:56,006
This photograph was taken at Charles de Gaulle, the airport it will never reach.

24
00:01:57,133 --> 00:02:00,618
The A330 is a fly by wire machine.

25
00:02:00,618 --> 00:02:08,896
Computers sit between the pilots and the wings, quietly smoothing every input, and making it almost impossible to stall.

26
00:02:08,896 --> 00:02:09,332
Almost.

27
00:02:10,466 --> 00:02:16,799
In the cockpit are three pilots. The captain, 58 years old, nearly 11,000 flight hours.

28
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:23,553
And two co-pilots, because a 10 hour overnight crossing requires the crew to sleep in shifts.

29
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:28,823
The junior of them is 32 years old, with 2,900 hours.

30
00:02:28,823 --> 00:02:31,822
He is fully qualified, and he loves flying.

31
00:02:31,822 --> 00:02:38,571
Tonight, for the crossing of the Atlantic, he will be the one with his hands on the controls.

32
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:42,081
Hold on to one number about him.

33
00:02:42,081 --> 00:02:47,524
In his entire career, almost all of his flying had been done with the autopilot engaged.

34
00:02:47,524 --> 00:02:52,287
Hand flying a jet at high altitude was something he had almost never done.

35
00:02:52,287 --> 00:02:53,989
Almost no airline pilot had.

36
00:02:55,133 --> 00:03:03,571
At 22:29 universal time, flight 447 lifts off into the night and turns north east, out over the Atlantic.

37
00:03:03,571 --> 00:03:09,344
For the first three and a half hours, everything is perfectly, completely routine.

38
00:03:10,466 --> 00:03:12,817
Chapter 2. The dead zone.

39
00:03:13,966 --> 00:03:19,332
There is a stretch of the South Atlantic where aircraft are truly alone.

40
00:03:19,332 --> 00:03:24,285
Beyond the reach of radar. Beyond normal radio. Controllers cannot see you.

41
00:03:24,285 --> 00:03:28,412
For hours, nobody on Earth knows exactly where you are.

42
00:03:29,533 --> 00:03:33,782
And across that stretch lies a permanent wall of weather.

43
00:03:33,782 --> 00:03:42,281
The intertropical convergence zone, where the trade winds of two hemispheres collide and pile thunderstorms miles high, every single night.

44
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:48,581
Towering cells like these stack up along the zone night after night.

45
00:03:48,581 --> 00:03:53,331
Investigators would later conclude the weather that night was not exceptional.

46
00:03:53,331 --> 00:03:58,081
Crews cross it constantly. Flight 447 was flying into something ordinary.

47
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:05,890
At 1:35 in the morning, the crew makes a routine radio call to the Brazilian controllers at Atlantico.

48
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:07,005
Position, altitude, estimates.

49
00:04:07,005 --> 00:04:12,209
It is the last time anyone on the ground will ever hear from them.

50
00:04:13,333 --> 00:04:16,380
At 2:01, the captain does something completely normal.

51
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:22,095
He leaves for his scheduled rest break, and hands the airplane to the two co-pilots.

52
00:04:22,095 --> 00:04:27,047
On an overnight crossing, this is standard on every airline in the world.

53
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:31,830
The junior co-pilot has the controls, in the right seat.

54
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:39,158
Ahead of them, painted in green and yellow on the weather radar, the storms of the convergence zone are waiting.

55
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:41,723
It is 8 minutes before the emergency.

56
00:04:42,866 --> 00:04:46,886
High in those clouds live ice crystals unlike anything at ground level.

57
00:04:46,886 --> 00:04:52,247
Swarms of them, too small for weather radar to see clearly, at temperatures far below freezing.

58
00:04:52,247 --> 00:04:56,267
And they have a taste for one particular part of an airplane.

59
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:58,418
The pitot tubes.

60
00:04:58,418 --> 00:05:03,512
Three small forward facing probes on the nose, that measure the speed of the air.

61
00:05:03,512 --> 00:05:09,286
Everything the computers know about how fast the plane is flying comes through these three little tubes.

62
00:05:10,433 --> 00:05:13,919
And here is the part the passengers never knew.

63
00:05:13,919 --> 00:05:18,954
These exact probes, on this exact model, had a known problem with ice.

64
00:05:18,954 --> 00:05:23,990
It had happened before. The airline was already replacing them, fleet by fleet.

65
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,430
The new probes were on their way. They arrived for this aircraft weeks too late.

66
00:05:30,430 --> 00:05:33,962
Flight 447 flew into the storm with the old ones.

67
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:38,653
Chapter 3. Four minutes and twenty-three seconds.

68
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,502
2:10 and 5 seconds, universal time.

69
00:05:42,502 --> 00:05:47,907
Inside the storm, ice crystals flood all three pitot tubes at once.

70
00:05:47,907 --> 00:05:49,709
The speed readings collapse.

71
00:05:49,709 --> 00:05:56,466
And the autopilot, suddenly blind, does exactly what it is designed to do. It quits.

72
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,849
A warning chime fills the cockpit.

73
00:05:59,849 --> 00:06:03,599
The flight controls degrade to what Airbus calls alternate law.

74
00:06:03,599 --> 00:06:10,348
In plain language, the computer protections that normally make this plane impossible to stall have just switched off.

75
00:06:11,466 --> 00:06:15,496
The junior co-pilot takes the controls, exactly as he should.

76
00:06:15,496 --> 00:06:17,915
Listen to how calm it starts.

77
00:06:17,915 --> 00:06:25,572
These cockpit exchanges are recreations, using the official words from the flight's voice recorder, published by the French investigators.

78
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:28,096
I have the controls.

79
00:06:29,233 --> 00:06:35,154
Two seconds later, he makes the input that will define the rest of his life.

80
00:06:35,154 --> 00:06:41,470
Startled, in turbulence, in the dark, he pulls back on his side stick. The nose rises.

81
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:43,444
The airplane begins to climb.

82
00:06:44,566 --> 00:06:49,962
Understand what has actually gone wrong here. Nothing. The engines are perfect.

83
00:06:49,962 --> 00:06:54,009
The wings are perfect. The plane is flying beautifully.

84
00:06:54,009 --> 00:06:59,404
Only the speed display is lying, and only for a few moments.

85
00:07:00,533 --> 00:07:03,278
We've lost the... the speeds.

86
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:06,870
The other co-pilot sees the problem.

87
00:07:06,870 --> 00:07:10,576
There is a written procedure for exactly this failure.

88
00:07:10,576 --> 00:07:15,517
Hold the wings level, set a known pitch and power, and wait.

89
00:07:15,517 --> 00:07:20,047
The plane will fly on happily. Nobody reaches for that procedure.

90
00:07:21,166 --> 00:07:28,149
Instead, the aircraft is now climbing hard, trading its speed for altitude it does not need.

91
00:07:28,149 --> 00:07:36,004
From 35,000 feet, it soars toward 38,000, its nose reaching upward, the air growing thinner around its wings.

92
00:07:37,133 --> 00:07:40,904
According to all three, you're going up. So go back down.

93
00:07:42,033 --> 00:07:48,513
He sees it. He says it. The airplane is climbing and it must come down.

94
00:07:48,513 --> 00:07:52,833
For a moment the nose lowers, the situation almost stabilizes.

95
00:07:52,833 --> 00:07:58,882
Within about a minute, the ice melts. The speed displays come back. True readings.

96
00:07:59,999 --> 00:08:04,247
Understand that. Barely a minute in, the original failure is over.

97
00:08:04,247 --> 00:08:08,494
The airplane is whole again. Every instrument is telling the truth.

98
00:08:08,494 --> 00:08:12,355
All they have to do now is nothing at all.

99
00:08:13,499 --> 00:08:17,421
But in the right seat, the pulling continues.

100
00:08:17,421 --> 00:08:22,813
Small pulls, nervous pulls, in turbulence, in darkness, with alarms chattering.

101
00:08:22,813 --> 00:08:30,165
The nose stays high. The speed bleeds away. And then the airplane says the word.

102
00:08:31,299 --> 00:08:33,990
Chapter 4. The word nobody said.

103
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:36,648
STALL. STALL. STALL.

104
00:08:36,648 --> 00:08:42,708
A synthetic voice, engineered to be impossible to ignore, fills the cockpit.

105
00:08:42,708 --> 00:08:50,284
It means one thing. The wings are about to stop flying. Push the nose down.

106
00:08:51,433 --> 00:08:56,711
Over the next four minutes, that warning will sound 75 separate times.

107
00:08:56,711 --> 00:09:01,110
At one point it blares continuously for 54 seconds straight.

108
00:09:01,110 --> 00:09:05,069
It is the loudest, clearest voice in the cockpit.

109
00:09:06,199 --> 00:09:14,538
And in everything the three pilots say to each other, on the entire recording, not one of them ever says the word stall.

110
00:09:14,538 --> 00:09:18,163
Not once. They can hear it. They never believe it.

111
00:09:19,299 --> 00:09:24,363
Why? Partly because they had been taught, correctly, that this airplane cannot stall.

112
00:09:24,363 --> 00:09:28,259
That was true, under normal law, with the protections on.

113
00:09:28,259 --> 00:09:32,543
The protections had been off for a minute and a half.

114
00:09:33,666 --> 00:09:36,711
At 38,000 feet, the wings finally give up.

115
00:09:36,711 --> 00:09:46,988
The angle of attack passes the point of no return, the smooth air over the wings tears away, and the Airbus, still nose high, begins to fall.

116
00:09:48,133 --> 00:09:49,527
I'm in TOGA, eh.

117
00:09:50,666 --> 00:09:57,760
He slams the thrust levers to takeoff power, the reflex every pilot learns for a stall near the ground.

118
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:03,734
But up here, in air this thin, the engines cannot save a wing this deeply stalled.

119
00:10:03,734 --> 00:10:05,974
Only the nose going down can.

120
00:10:07,099 --> 00:10:12,649
But we've got the engines. What's happening? Do you understand what's happening, or not?

121
00:10:13,766 --> 00:10:18,841
The aircraft is now falling like nothing either man has ever experienced.

122
00:10:18,841 --> 00:10:24,761
Ten thousand feet per minute, nose held high, wings rocking, alarms layered on alarms.

123
00:10:24,761 --> 00:10:29,413
Their instruments show readings so extreme they refuse to believe them.

124
00:10:30,533 --> 00:10:33,152
I don't have control of the airplane any more now.

125
00:10:33,152 --> 00:10:35,509
I don't have control of the airplane at all.

126
00:10:36,633 --> 00:10:40,185
In those two sentences lives the whole tragedy.

127
00:10:40,185 --> 00:10:45,958
Because the recorder shows the airplane obeying him, faithfully, to the very end.

128
00:10:45,958 --> 00:10:49,955
He has control. What he has lost is understanding.

129
00:10:51,099 --> 00:10:54,025
Chapter 5. The captain returns.

130
00:10:55,166 --> 00:10:59,546
One minute and 38 seconds into the emergency, the cockpit door opens.

131
00:10:59,546 --> 00:11:03,562
The captain is back, dragged from his rest by the alarms.

132
00:11:03,562 --> 00:11:09,037
He stands behind the two seats, looking at an airplane falling out of the sky.

133
00:11:10,166 --> 00:11:11,647
Er... what are you doing?

134
00:11:12,766 --> 00:11:17,693
We've lost all control of the aeroplane. We don't understand anything.

135
00:11:17,693 --> 00:11:19,037
We've tried everything.

136
00:11:20,166 --> 00:11:22,509
Put yourself in the captain's place.

137
00:11:22,509 --> 00:11:26,804
He has been out of the cockpit for the entire emergency.

138
00:11:26,804 --> 00:11:34,612
He arrives to a wall of alarms, two frightened co-pilots, instruments showing the impossible, and 30 seconds to diagnose it.

139
00:11:35,733 --> 00:11:40,522
And the airplane itself is lying to him in the cruelest way possible.

140
00:11:40,522 --> 00:11:47,890
Because of how the warning logic works, at the speeds they have now reached, the stall warning has fallen silent.

141
00:11:47,890 --> 00:11:50,100
The computers consider the data invalid.

142
00:11:51,233 --> 00:11:51,603
Worse.

143
00:11:51,603 --> 00:12:00,854
When the flying pilot briefly pushes the nose down, the correct action, the speed readings become valid again, and the stall warning comes BACK ON.

144
00:12:00,854 --> 00:12:04,555
Doing the right thing sounds like doing the wrong thing.

145
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:08,211
The investigators would call this out directly.

146
00:12:08,211 --> 00:12:16,824
The one instrument that could have told them the truth in one glance, an angle of attack display, was not installed in this cockpit.

147
00:12:16,824 --> 00:12:19,336
It existed. It simply was not there.

148
00:12:20,466 --> 00:12:26,013
I have the impression we have some crazy speed. No? What do you think?

149
00:12:27,133 --> 00:12:29,784
He believes the opposite of the truth.

150
00:12:29,784 --> 00:12:40,769
He thinks the aircraft is overspeeding, diving too fast, when it is actually falling in a deep stall, mushing through the air at barely 100 knots of forward speed.

151
00:12:41,899 --> 00:12:47,160
For two more minutes, three intelligent, trained men stand inside a paradox.

152
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:49,352
Every instinct contradicts every instrument.

153
00:12:49,352 --> 00:12:55,927
The ocean is coming up at them through the dark at 180 feet every second.

154
00:12:57,066 --> 00:12:59,247
But I've been at maxi nose-up for a while!

155
00:13:00,366 --> 00:13:01,592
There it is.

156
00:13:01,592 --> 00:13:07,718
The sentence that finally explains everything, spoken 2 minutes and 35 seconds into the fall.

157
00:13:07,718 --> 00:13:12,211
He has been holding the stick back almost the entire time.

158
00:13:12,211 --> 00:13:13,845
The captain understands instantly.

159
00:13:14,966 --> 00:13:17,262
No, no, no... don't climb.

160
00:13:18,399 --> 00:13:23,088
The other co-pilot takes the controls and pushes the nose down.

161
00:13:23,088 --> 00:13:26,924
The wings begin, slowly, to find the air again.

162
00:13:26,924 --> 00:13:32,464
But they are passing through 9,000 feet, falling at 180 feet per second.

163
00:13:32,464 --> 00:13:34,595
The mathematics are already written.

164
00:13:35,733 --> 00:13:40,384
We're going to crash. This can't be true. But what's happening?

165
00:13:41,533 --> 00:13:46,124
The last words on the recording belong to the captain.

166
00:13:46,124 --> 00:13:51,633
Calm, professional, still flying to the final second. Ten degrees, pitch attitude.

167
00:13:51,633 --> 00:13:56,684
At 2 hours, 14 minutes and 28 seconds, the recording ends.

168
00:13:57,833 --> 00:14:04,735
Air France 447 hit the ocean flat, belly first, at more than 10,000 feet per minute.

169
00:14:04,735 --> 00:14:12,931
It had fallen 38,000 feet in under 4 minutes, engines running perfectly, in an aircraft that was never broken.

170
00:14:12,931 --> 00:14:13,794
Nobody survived.

171
00:14:14,933 --> 00:14:17,859
Chapter 6. Twenty-four messages.

172
00:14:18,999 --> 00:14:25,325
Here is the part few people know. When flight 447 hit the water, nobody noticed.

173
00:14:25,325 --> 00:14:29,542
No radar saw it fall. No radio heard a mayday.

174
00:14:29,542 --> 00:14:33,759
The ocean closed over it, and the night simply continued.

175
00:14:34,899 --> 00:14:37,566
But the aircraft had left a trail.

176
00:14:37,566 --> 00:14:43,661
As it fell, its maintenance computer did what it was built to do. It phoned home.

177
00:14:43,661 --> 00:14:48,613
24 automated fault messages, fired by satellite to Air France operations in Paris.

178
00:14:49,733 --> 00:14:54,020
Fourteen messages in the first minute alone. Autopilot off.

179
00:14:54,020 --> 00:15:01,165
Flight controls in alternate law. Air data faults. And then the last one, at 2:14.

180
00:15:01,165 --> 00:15:05,929
Cabin vertical speed advisory. The cabin was descending too fast.

181
00:15:07,066 --> 00:15:09,596
Think about what that last message is.

182
00:15:09,596 --> 00:15:13,932
The airplane, reporting its own fall, in its final second of life.

183
00:15:13,932 --> 00:15:20,075
For the next two years, these 24 lines of text would be the only testimony that existed.

184
00:15:21,199 --> 00:15:24,471
In Paris, it is the middle of the night.

185
00:15:24,471 --> 00:15:28,107
The messages land in a maintenance queue, waiting for morning.

186
00:15:28,107 --> 00:15:34,286
When the flight fails to check in with air traffic control, confusion spreads slowly between control centers.

187
00:15:35,433 --> 00:15:42,447
The official distress phase was not declared until 6 and a half hours after the aircraft hit the water.

188
00:15:42,447 --> 00:15:49,461
For all that time, the world assumed flight 447 was simply flying, silent and unseen, across the dead zone.

189
00:15:50,599 --> 00:15:56,148
Brazilian Air Force planes swept the ocean for days and found nothing but water.

190
00:15:56,148 --> 00:16:02,489
An Airbus A330 is 60 meters long. The search area was the size of a country.

191
00:16:02,489 --> 00:16:04,470
It had vanished into it.

192
00:16:05,599 --> 00:16:10,056
On the fifth day, they found it. Seat cushions. Luggage.

193
00:16:10,056 --> 00:16:20,750
And then, lifted dripping from the swell by the Brazilian Navy, the aircraft's vertical stabilizer, painted in the red and blue of Air France.

194
00:16:21,899 --> 00:16:23,540
This is the actual photograph.

195
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:29,445
That piece of the tail became the image of the disaster, printed on every front page on Earth.

196
00:16:29,445 --> 00:16:33,053
50 victims were recovered from the surface in those first weeks.

197
00:16:34,199 --> 00:16:40,984
In Rio and Paris, families waited at the Air France counters as the airline confirmed the impossible.

198
00:16:40,984 --> 00:16:44,576
228 names. Now the question that would consume France.

199
00:16:44,576 --> 00:16:48,566
Why does a perfect airliner fall out of the sky?

200
00:16:49,699 --> 00:16:51,972
Chapter 7. The abyss.

201
00:16:53,099 --> 00:16:58,372
The answer lay in two orange boxes, somewhere at the bottom of the Atlantic.

202
00:16:58,372 --> 00:17:01,761
The flight data recorder and the cockpit voice recorder.

203
00:17:01,761 --> 00:17:06,656
Without them, the 24 messages were clues. With them, there would be truth.

204
00:17:07,799 --> 00:17:12,995
But the seabed here is not flat. It is a mountain range.

205
00:17:12,995 --> 00:17:18,624
Peaks and canyons, 4 kilometers down, in blackness no light has ever touched.

206
00:17:18,624 --> 00:17:25,118
The boxes' locator beacons pinged for 30 days, into water too deep to hear them.

207
00:17:26,266 --> 00:17:31,342
France sent a nuclear submarine. Ships dragged sonar for months.

208
00:17:31,342 --> 00:17:35,911
A first search found nothing. A second found nothing.

209
00:17:35,911 --> 00:17:41,495
A third, guided by drift calculations, found nothing. Two years passed.

210
00:17:42,633 --> 00:17:47,098
The families began to fear the truth would never surface.

211
00:17:47,098 --> 00:17:52,010
Conspiracy theories bloomed in the vacuum. Terrorism. Lightning. A cover up.

212
00:17:52,010 --> 00:17:56,922
Without the boxes, every theory was as good as any other.

213
00:17:58,066 --> 00:18:08,456
For the fourth attempt, in 2011, the investigators hired the deep ocean group at Woods Hole, the American institution whose people had found the Titanic.

214
00:18:08,456 --> 00:18:11,780
They brought three of these. REMUS 6000 robots.

215
00:18:12,899 --> 00:18:20,800
Torpedo shaped, autonomous, diving 20 hours at a time, flying search patterns over the abyssal plain like tireless bloodhounds.

216
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:28,285
And in the first week of April 2011, on a flat plain between the mountains, there it was.

217
00:18:29,433 --> 00:18:34,239
A debris field, 3,900 meters down. Engines. Landing gear. Wing sections.

218
00:18:34,239 --> 00:18:46,909
The aircraft had come down largely intact and shattered on the water, and its pieces had rained down onto the seabed, nearly on top of its last known position.

219
00:18:48,033 --> 00:18:53,780
On May 1st, 2011, a robotic arm lifted the flight data recorder from the silt.

220
00:18:53,780 --> 00:18:56,079
Two days later, the voice recorder.

221
00:18:56,079 --> 00:19:01,826
Twenty-three months after the crash, hauled up from a depth greater than the Titanic's grave.

222
00:19:02,966 --> 00:19:06,876
104 more victims were brought home from the wreck that spring.

223
00:19:06,876 --> 00:19:10,785
74 remain in the Atlantic, at the site, to this day.

224
00:19:10,785 --> 00:19:14,695
Their families asked that the sea be left as their grave.

225
00:19:15,833 --> 00:19:23,049
In a laboratory outside Paris, engineers dried the memory boards, connected them, and pressed play.

226
00:19:23,049 --> 00:19:26,898
Both recorders had survived. Every parameter, every word.

227
00:19:26,898 --> 00:19:33,152
For the first time, the investigators heard the night of June 1st, 2009.

228
00:19:34,299 --> 00:19:36,990
Chapter 8. What the boxes said.

229
00:19:38,133 --> 00:19:43,184
The French investigation bureau, the BEA, published its final report in July 2012.

230
00:19:43,184 --> 00:19:47,458
It is one of the most consequential documents in aviation history.

231
00:19:47,458 --> 00:19:50,567
And it refuses to tell the simple story.

232
00:19:51,699 --> 00:19:56,759
The simple story is: the pilot pulled up when he should have pushed down.

233
00:19:56,759 --> 00:19:59,288
It is true, and it explains nothing.

234
00:19:59,288 --> 00:20:04,708
The report instead asks the only question that matters. Why did trained pilots do that?

235
00:20:05,833 --> 00:20:09,013
The chain begins with the pitot probes.

236
00:20:09,013 --> 00:20:17,192
In the report's words, blockage by ice crystals was a known phenomenon, but misunderstood by the aviation community.

237
00:20:17,192 --> 00:20:20,827
Known. Already being fixed. Not fixed fast enough.

238
00:20:21,966 --> 00:20:23,135
Then the startle.

239
00:20:23,135 --> 00:20:31,708
The report describes a crew completely surprised, at night, in weather, by an alarm cascade they had never faced outside a classroom.

240
00:20:31,708 --> 00:20:35,994
The emergency began with fear, and fear never left the cockpit.

241
00:20:37,133 --> 00:20:38,755
Then the training gap.

242
00:20:38,755 --> 00:20:47,679
The flying pilot had almost no experience hand flying at high altitude, because the industry had decided automation made that skill obsolete.

243
00:20:47,679 --> 00:20:51,735
When the automation left, it took his world with it.

244
00:20:52,866 --> 00:20:54,642
Then the machine's own psychology.

245
00:20:54,642 --> 00:21:01,034
A stall warning that fell silent in the deepest stall, and returned when he did the right thing.

246
00:21:01,034 --> 00:21:02,810
No angle of attack gauge.

247
00:21:02,810 --> 00:21:07,782
Two side sticks that move independently, so neither pilot feels what the other commands.

248
00:21:08,899 --> 00:21:10,985
That last one deserves a moment.

249
00:21:10,985 --> 00:21:18,634
Through much of the fall, the two pilots were making OPPOSITE inputs, one pushing, one pulling, and the sticks never told them.

250
00:21:18,634 --> 00:21:23,501
In an older aircraft with linked controls, each would have felt the other's hands.

251
00:21:24,633 --> 00:21:27,265
The report's conclusion, in its own words.

252
00:21:27,265 --> 00:21:33,657
The crew never understood they were in a stall situation, and therefore never undertook any recovery manoeuvres.

253
00:21:33,657 --> 00:21:38,922
They did not fail to save the plane. They never knew it needed saving.

254
00:21:40,066 --> 00:21:53,232
And so the deaths of 228 people trace back, link by link, to three iced tubes, one startled pull, a warning system that lied by design, and an industry that had quietly stopped teaching pilots to fly.

255
00:21:54,366 --> 00:21:57,213
Chapter 9. The longest verdict.

256
00:21:58,333 --> 00:22:03,836
The report changed flying. Pitot probes were replaced across the world fleet.

257
00:22:03,836 --> 00:22:07,046
Stall recovery training was rewritten from scratch.

258
00:22:07,046 --> 00:22:10,715
High altitude manual flying returned to the simulator.

259
00:22:10,715 --> 00:22:14,842
Every airline pilot today trains differently because of 447.

260
00:22:15,966 --> 00:22:22,605
But for the families, one question stayed open. Not what happened. Who answers for it.

261
00:22:22,605 --> 00:22:27,474
The probes were a known problem. The training gap was known.

262
00:22:27,474 --> 00:22:31,457
Somebody, somewhere, had accepted those risks on their behalf.

263
00:22:32,599 --> 00:22:38,370
It took 13 years to bring Air France and Airbus into a criminal courtroom.

264
00:22:38,370 --> 00:22:43,316
In October 2022, in Paris, both companies stood trial for involuntary manslaughter.

265
00:22:43,316 --> 00:22:47,437
The first trial of its kind in French aviation history.

266
00:22:48,566 --> 00:22:52,432
In April 2023, the verdict. Acquitted. Both companies.

267
00:22:52,432 --> 00:23:02,581
The court found negligence, but said it could not prove, to the criminal standard, that those failures directly caused the crash.

268
00:23:02,581 --> 00:23:04,997
Families wept in the courtroom.

269
00:23:06,133 --> 00:23:11,061
That is where this story stood for years. It is where most tellings of it end.

270
00:23:11,061 --> 00:23:13,526
But it is not where it ends now.

271
00:23:14,666 --> 00:23:19,815
In May 2026, the Paris Court of Appeal overturned the acquittal.

272
00:23:19,815 --> 00:23:24,028
Air France, convicted, for failures in training its crews.

273
00:23:24,028 --> 00:23:30,581
Airbus, convicted, for failing to act on the known danger of its speed probes.

274
00:23:30,581 --> 00:23:31,985
Corporate manslaughter, both.

275
00:23:33,133 --> 00:23:39,413
The fine, the maximum French law allows a company for this crime, was 225,000 euros each.

276
00:23:39,413 --> 00:23:42,946
Less than the price of one business class cabin.

277
00:23:42,946 --> 00:23:47,657
The families called the number an insult, and the verdict a victory.

278
00:23:48,799 --> 00:23:51,588
Airbus is appealing to France's highest court.

279
00:23:51,588 --> 00:23:59,954
Seventeen years after the tail fin was lifted from the Atlantic, the case of Air France 447 is, legally, still falling.

280
00:24:01,099 --> 00:24:11,436
228 people died in an unbroken airplane, because three small tubes iced over for less than a minute, and everything human that surrounded that moment failed at once.

281
00:24:11,436 --> 00:24:15,127
The machine was fine. The system around it was not.

282
00:24:16,266 --> 00:24:22,381
Since that night, no passenger has died in a crash caused by the same chain.

283
00:24:22,381 --> 00:24:25,642
The probes, the training, the warnings, all rebuilt.

284
00:24:25,642 --> 00:24:31,757
That is the grim arithmetic of aviation. Safety rules are written in exactly this ink.

285
00:24:32,900 --> 00:24:47,189
If this story gripped you, watch our breakdown of Colgan Air 3407 next, the crash where two exhausted pilots made the same fatal pull, at 2,000 feet instead of 38,000, and changed American law forever.

286
00:24:48,333 --> 00:24:50,088
And subscribe to Black Box.

287
00:24:50,088 --> 00:24:56,758
We take the biggest disasters in history apart, piece by piece, to show you the decisions hiding inside them.

288
00:24:56,758 --> 00:24:59,566
Because the truth is always in the details.

